Hi David,

thanks for your e-mail. Your Asciiverse projects looks really neat! You
have my full permission and consent to use my games for this purpose. Note,
however, that some of the games are not strictly grid-based: while they
have a tty-like look, they often leverage HTML-based UI elements or
non-rectangular layout (The Conception, for instance, draws hexagonal
cells).
I might eventually add the proper licensing (MIT sounds like a solid
choice), but for now, please use this e-mail as a proof of my compliance.


Sincerely,
Ondrej Zara



p=C3=A1 13. 11. 2026 v 17:16 odes=C3=ADlatel David Sandquist <sandquist@gma=
il.com>
napsal:

> Hi Ond=C5=99ej,
>
> Thank you for rot.js, and for more than a decade of 7DRL games. A good
> share of the web's roguelikes stand on your toolkit, and your own games a=
re
> some of the nicest of them.
>
> I run Asciiverse (https://asciiverse.sh), a free catalog and launcher for
> terminal games, which opened this month. A small client, av, shows each
> game's recorded preview, then installs and starts it in whatever terminal
> you already have, on Linux, macOS and Windows. It's open source, with no
> accounts and everything free. The catalog holds 296 titles so far, from
> Hunt the Wumpus and Rogue to NetHack and games made this year, each
> credited to its authors with its licence kept.
>
> I would love to bring some of your games to the terminal. They are alread=
y
> drawn as character grids, so they belong there naturally. Warden's Duty,
> Rogue Zombies and The Great Train Robbery carry licences, so those can go
> ahead as they are. These have none in their repositories:
>
> - The Royal Wedding
> - Sleeping Beauty
> - Goldfish
> - Caf=C3=A9 Havoc
> - The Conception
> - Rogue Sea
> - The Curse of the General Sibling Combinator
>
> Would you be willing to add a licence to them, MIT as on your newer games=
,
> or simply give us permission to convert them? Your name, your game and it=
s
> credits would stay up front, and the terminal versions would be open sour=
ce
> like the rest of the catalog.
>
> If you would rather some of them stay as they are, that is of course fine=
;
> I would just like to ask before touching any of them.
>
> Best regards,
> David
> Asciiverse
> Asciiverse
>

